    id: 940,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The placebo effect is a well-documented phenomenon in which patients experience real improvements in their condition after receiving a treatment that has no therapeutic active ingredient. The effect is not merely psychological—placebos have been shown to produce measurable physiological changes, including the release of endorphins (natural painkillers) and dopamine, and changes in heart rate and blood pressure. The strength of the placebo effect varies depending on several factors. The size and color of placebo pills matter: larger pills produce stronger effects than smaller ones, and colored pills work better than white ones. Injections produce stronger placebo effects than pills. The doctor's bedside manner also plays a role: warm, confident, and attentive physicians elicit stronger placebo responses. Interestingly, the placebo effect appears to be getting stronger over time in clinical trials in the United States, possibly because patients have higher expectations of treatment efficacy due to direct-to-consumer advertising of pharmaceuticals.",
