    id: 941,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The Silk Road was not a single road but a vast network of trade routes connecting East Asia with the Mediterranean world, spanning over 6,000 kilometers. It operated from approximately 130 BCE, when the Han dynasty of China established official trade relations with Central Asia, until the fifteenth century, when maritime trade routes began to supplant overland routes. While silk was the most famous commodity traded along these routes, the exchange of goods was remarkably diverse: spices, textiles, precious stones, glassware, and ceramics all moved along the network. Perhaps more significantly, the Silk Road facilitated the exchange of ideas, technologies, and religions. Buddhism spread from India to China via these routes. Papermaking, gunpowder, and the compass—all Chinese inventions—gradually reached the West. The Silk Road also facilitated the transmission of diseases, most notably the bubonic plague, which traveled from Asia to Europe along the trade routes and contributed to the Black Death pandemic of the fourteenth century.",
