    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The study of epigenetics has revealed that the environment can influence gene expression without changing the underlying DNA sequence. Epigenetic modifications, such as DNA methylation and histone modification, act as a molecular switchboard that turns genes on or off in response to environmental signals. For example, studies of honeybees have shown that genetically identical larvae develop into either queens or workers depending on whether they are fed royal jelly, which triggers epigenetic changes that activate queen-specific genes. Perhaps the most striking evidence comes from studies of monozygotic (identical) twins. While twins are born with the same epigenome, differences accumulate over their lifetimes as they are exposed to different environments, diets, and stresses. These epigenetic differences help explain why one twin may develop a disease while the other does not, despite sharing the same genetic code. The discovery that environmental factors can produce stable, heritable changes in gene expression has profound implications for our understanding of inheritance, disease, and evolution.",
