    id: 943,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The concept of 'creative destruction,' introduced by economist Joseph Schumpeter in 1942, describes the process by which innovation revolutionizes economic structures from within, destroying old industries and creating new ones. For Schumpeter, this process was the essential driving force of capitalism. The automobile industry destroyed the horse-and-buggy economy; the personal computer destroyed the typewriter and mainframe markets; and streaming services are now destroying traditional television and physical media. Schumpeter argued that the entrepreneur is the agent of creative destruction—the individual who introduces new combinations that disrupt existing equilibria. Unlike other economists of his time, who focused on how markets reach equilibrium, Schumpeter emphasized how capitalism is perpetually in a state of disequilibrium, with growth driven by the constant churn of innovation. He believed that the benefits of creative destruction to society as a whole—cheaper products, new technologies, economic growth—outweigh the costs to displaced industries and workers. Critics, however, note that Schumpeter may have underestimated the social costs of displacement and the difficulty that displaced workers face in transitioning to new industries.",
