    passage: "The Spitzer Space Telescope, launched in 2003, was designed to observe the universe in infrared light. Because infrared radiation is primarily heat radiation, the telescope had to be kept extremely cold to prevent its own heat from interfering with its observations. Spitzer's original supply of liquid helium coolant kept its instruments at minus 268 degrees Celsius—just five degrees above absolute zero. This coolant lasted for about five and a half years, after which the telescope entered the 'warm mission' phase. Even without active cooling, some of Spitzer's instruments continued to operate effectively at the warmer temperature of minus 243 degrees Celsius because they were designed to observe infrared wavelengths that were less affected by the telescope's own heat. The mission continued for another decade, demonstrating that careful design can extend the useful life of space observatories beyond their original coolant supply.",
