    id: 962,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The economist Amartya Sen's 'capabilities approach' to development challenges the traditional view that economic growth is the ultimate measure of a society's progress. Sen argues that what matters is not how much wealth a society produces, but what people are actually able to do and be—their capabilities. These capabilities include being able to live a long and healthy life, to be educated, to participate in political life, and to have freedom of movement and expression. According to Sen, income and wealth are merely means to these ends, not ends in themselves. A society that achieves high GDP growth but fails to provide adequate healthcare, education, and political freedoms for its citizens is not, in Sen's view, truly developed. This approach has influenced the United Nations Human Development Index, which measures countries not just by income but also by life expectancy and education levels.",
