    passage: "The coelacanth is a fish that was thought to have gone extinct 66 million years ago, until a living specimen was caught off the coast of South Africa in 1938. This discovery is often described as finding a living dinosaur, but the comparison is misleading in important ways. Coelacanths are not the ancestors of any land-dwelling vertebrates; they are a side branch of the evolutionary tree that has remained remarkably unchanged for hundreds of millions of years. The modern coelacanth differs from its fossil ancestors in several respects, including a larger brain size and differences in skull structure. The living coelacanth is also found in deep water—between 100 and 500 meters below the surface—whereas fossil coelacanths have been found in a variety of shallow-water and freshwater deposits. These differences suggest that the coelacanth has not simply been 'frozen in time' but has continued to evolve, albeit slowly and in ways that preserved its overall body plan.",
