    id: 964,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The Dunning-Kruger effect is a cognitive bias in which people with low ability at a task overestimate their ability, while people with high ability underestimate their competence. The effect was first described by psychologists David Dunning and Justin Kruger in 1999, based on studies of college students' performance in logic, grammar, and humor. They found that students in the bottom quartile of performance rated their skills far higher than objective measures warranted, while those in the top quartile tended to underestimate their relative standing. Dunning and Kruger attributed this asymmetry to a double burden faced by the unskilled: not only do they perform poorly, but their lack of skill also deprives them of the metacognitive ability to recognize their poor performance. In other words, the knowledge needed to perform well is the same knowledge needed to recognize competence. Subsequent research has complicated the original findings, with some studies suggesting that the effect may be weaker in domains where people receive clear, immediate feedback on their performance.",
