    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The phenomenon of 'biological magnification' describes how certain toxins become more concentrated as they move up the food chain. When a persistent chemical such as DDT or mercury enters an ecosystem, it is absorbed by primary producers like algae and phytoplankton in very low concentrations. Small fish that eat large quantities of these producers accumulate the toxin in their tissues because they absorb it faster than they can excrete it. When larger fish eat many small fish, the toxin concentration becomes even higher. At the top of the food chain, apex predators such as eagles, bears, and humans can have toxin concentrations millions of times higher than those found in the environment. This is why the bald eagle nearly went extinct in the mid-twentieth century: DDT, which was sprayed on crops in low concentrations, accumulated in fish to levels that caused eagle eggshells to become dangerously thin.",
