    passage: "The Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, also known as linguistic relativity, proposes that the structure of a language influences how its speakers perceive and think about the world. The strong version of the hypothesis—that language determines thought—has been largely rejected by linguists, but a weaker version—that language influences thought—has received considerable empirical support. For example, speakers of languages that use absolute directional terms (north, south, east, west) rather than relative terms (left, right) have been shown to have superior navigational abilities and a heightened awareness of their orientation at all times. Studies of color perception have found that speakers of languages with more color terms can distinguish between shades more quickly than speakers of languages with fewer color terms. However, critics argue that many of these studies demonstrate correlation rather than causation: it may be that cultural or environmental factors, rather than language itself, drive the observed differences in cognition.",
