    passage: "The Fermi Paradox addresses the apparent contradiction between the high probability of extraterrestrial life, given the vast number of stars in the universe, and the absence of any evidence for it. Enrico Fermi famously asked, 'If the universe is teeming with intelligent life, where is everybody?' Several proposed solutions to the paradox have been advanced. The 'Great Filter' hypothesis suggests that there is a stage in the development of life that is extremely difficult to pass through—whether it is the emergence of life itself, the development of multicellular organisms, or the transition to a technologically advanced civilization. If the filter is behind us—meaning that early life is the hard part—then we may be one of the only civilizations to have made it through. If the filter is ahead of us—such as self-destruction through nuclear war or environmental collapse—then our future is grim. Other proposed solutions include the possibility that advanced civilizations are deliberately hiding from us (the 'zoo hypothesis') or that they simply do not last long enough to make contact across cosmic distances.",
