    passage: "The Stanford prison experiment, conducted by psychologist Philip Zimbardo in 1971, randomly assigned college students to play the roles of prisoners and guards in a mock prison set up in the basement of a Stanford University building. The experiment was scheduled to last two weeks but was terminated after only six days because the guards became increasingly abusive and the prisoners showed signs of severe psychological distress. Zimbardo concluded that the situation—the prison environment and the power dynamics inherent in the roles—overwhelmed the individual dispositions of the participants, causing ordinary, healthy college students to behave in ways that were cruel or submissive. The experiment has been widely cited as evidence of the power of situational factors to influence behavior. However, the experiment has also been subject to significant criticism. Critics have pointed out that Zimbardo himself played an active role as the prison superintendent, potentially influencing the behavior of the guards, and that the participants may have been acting in ways they believed were expected of them rather than responding naturally to the situation.",
